      "text": "To calculate the product \\(\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\\), we start by writing out the general term of the product:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k}{k+3} \\text{ for } k = 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 50.\n\\]\n\nThus, the product can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}.\n\\]\n\nWe can express this product as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdots 50}{4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdots 53}.\n\\]\n\nThe numerator is the product of the first 50 positive integers, which is \\(50!\\) (50 factorial). The denominator is the product of the first 53 positive integers starting from 4, which can be written as \\(4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdots 53\\). Notice that \\(4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdots 53\\) can be expressed as \\(\\frac{53!}{3!}\\) because:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdots 53 = \\frac{53!}{3!}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{50!}{\\frac{53!}{3!}} = \\frac{50! \\cdot 3!}{53!} = \\frac{3! \\cdot 50!}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50!} = \\frac{6 \\cdot 50!}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50!} = \\frac{6}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the denominator, we get:\n\n\\[\n53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 = 140412.\n\\]\n\nThus, the product is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{140412} = \\frac{1}{23402}.\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{23402}}.\n\\]",
